note as DEP_diagram_65_surveys
  Connects to other modules via:
  tenants, practitioner_profiles, patient_profiles, appointment_bookings,
  catalog_concepts, users
end note

note as REC_TARGET_SURVEYS_diagram_65_surveys
  REC 65.1 — THE ASSIGNMENT TARGET IS POLYMORPHIC, ON PURPOSE
survey_assignments.target_id carries the identity of whatever is being
evaluated — a concrete booking, a health service or a care type — and which one
it is depends on target_type_concept_id. It is the only uuid column in the
module with NO foreign key, and that is deliberate: three nullable columns with
a check that exactly one is set would say the same thing and make every read
branch. The pair (type, id) is the contract; nothing in the database enforces
that the id exists, so the service must resolve it against the schema the type
names. Revisit if a fourth target type appears.
end note

note as REC_PRIVACY_SURVEYS_diagram_65_surveys
  REC 65.2 — ANSWERS ARE PRIVATE BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY FLAG
survey_responses has no visibility column and survey_answers has no publication
state, and that absence is the design (decision D-08): an individual answer is
never publishable. What a visitor reads about a professional is
community.service_reviews, a different table with its own moderation. Adding a
"public" flag here would put the two behind one row and make a moderation bug a
privacy incident. patient_profile_id is duplicated from the invitation on
purpose, so a patient's own history can be read without joining invitations.
end note

note as REC_VALUE_SURVEYS_diagram_65_surveys
  REC 65.3 — ONE value_* COLUMN PER ROW, ENFORCED BY THE SERVICE
survey_answers keeps value_text, value_number, value_boolean and value_choices
all nullable, and exactly one of them is populated according to the question's
answer_type_concept_id. The entity documents that the SERVICE enforces it while
the module README claims a CHECK in the database — they contradict each other
and the code has no CHECK. This diagram transcribes the code: four nullable
columns, no constraint. Promoting the rule to a CHECK is a decision for the
model owner, not something to infer from prose.
end note

note as REC_IDEMPOTENCY_SURVEYS_diagram_65_surveys
  REC 65.4 — ISSUE IDEMPOTENCY IS NOT BACKED BY A CONSTRAINT YET
The module README states that issuing is idempotent, "unique per (booking,
version), also held by an index in the database". No such index exists: not in
the entities, not in the ORM catalogue, not in SQL. Today only
InvitationsRepository.listByBooking holds it, which is a read-then-write and
therefore loses a race. The unique index over survey_invitations
(appointment_booking_id, survey_version_id) is NOT declared here because adding
it would change behaviour — it would start rejecting rows that today are
accepted — and that is a decision, not a transcription. Same for the implicit
uniqueness of (survey_template_id, version_number) and of
survey_responses.survey_invitation_id, both assumed by findOne calls.
end note

note as REC_VERSION_SURVEYS_diagram_65_surveys
  REC 65.5 — PUBLISHED VERSIONS ARE IMMUTABLE, DRAFTS ARE NOT
A version whose publication_status_concept_id is SURVEY_VERSION_PUBLISHED must
not change, nor may its questions: correcting the instrument means publishing a
new version, which is what version_number and effective_from/effective_to are
for. The row is NOT marked <<IMMUTABLE>> because while it is a draft it does
mutate — hence row_version. The transition is what has to be guarded, and today
that lives in the service.
end note

package "Physical Indexes" {
entity "INDEXES · survey_templates" as idxset_survey_templates <<INDEX_SET>> {
  PK pk_survey_templates : (id) UNIQUE BTREE
  IX ix_survey_templates_owner : (owner_practitioner_id, created_at DESC) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_templates_tenant_id : (tenant_id) BTREE
}

survey_templates .. idxset_survey_templates : indexed by

entity "INDEXES · survey_versions" as idxset_survey_versions <<INDEX_SET>> {
  PK pk_survey_versions : (id) UNIQUE BTREE
  IX ix_survey_versions_template_number : (survey_template_id, version_number) BTREE
}

survey_versions .. idxset_survey_versions : indexed by

entity "INDEXES · survey_questions" as idxset_survey_questions <<INDEX_SET>> {
  PK pk_survey_questions : (id) UNIQUE BTREE
  IX ix_survey_questions_version_position : (survey_version_id, position) BTREE
}

survey_questions .. idxset_survey_questions : indexed by

entity "INDEXES · survey_assignments" as idxset_survey_assignments <<INDEX_SET>> {
  PK pk_survey_assignments : (id) UNIQUE BTREE
  IX ix_survey_assignments_tenant_target : (tenant_id, target_id, active) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_assignments_version : (survey_version_id) BTREE
}

survey_assignments .. idxset_survey_assignments : indexed by

entity "INDEXES · survey_invitations" as idxset_survey_invitations <<INDEX_SET>> {
  PK pk_survey_invitations : (id) UNIQUE BTREE
  IX ix_survey_invitations_patient_issued : (patient_profile_id, issued_at DESC) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_invitations_booking : (appointment_booking_id) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_invitations_version : (survey_version_id) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_invitations_assignment : (survey_assignment_id) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_invitations_tenant_id : (tenant_id) BTREE
}

survey_invitations .. idxset_survey_invitations : indexed by

entity "INDEXES · survey_responses" as idxset_survey_responses <<INDEX_SET>> {
  PK pk_survey_responses : (id) UNIQUE BTREE
  IX ix_survey_responses_invitation : (survey_invitation_id) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_responses_patient_submitted : (patient_profile_id, submitted_at DESC) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_responses_version : (survey_version_id) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_responses_tenant_id : (tenant_id) BTREE
}

survey_responses .. idxset_survey_responses : indexed by

entity "INDEXES · survey_answers" as idxset_survey_answers <<INDEX_SET>> {
  PK pk_survey_answers : (id) UNIQUE BTREE
  IX ix_survey_answers_response : (survey_response_id) BTREE
  IX ix_survey_answers_question : (survey_question_id) BTREE
}

survey_answers .. idxset_survey_answers : indexed by
}

@enduml
